92186045417 true] [17592186412996 1075 #edb/ref 17592186412992 13194139534170 17592186045417 true] [17592186412996 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139534170 17592186045417 true] [17592186412996 1077 "selection" 13194139534170 17592186045417 true] [17592186412996 1078 #edb/ref 17592186412992 13194139534170 17592186045417 true] [17592186412996 1079 #uuid "d49a1696-245b-5c6e-b7fe-d2569b597279" 13194139534170 17592186045417 true] [17592186412997 1075 #edb/ref 17592186412983 13194139534170 17592186045417 true] [17592186412997 1075 #edb/ref 17592186412984 13194139534170 17592186045417 true] [17592186412997 1075 #edb/ref 17592186412985 13194139534170 17592186045417 true] [17592186412997 1075 #edb/ref 17592186412986 13194139534170 17592186045417 true] [17592186412997 1075 #edb/ref 17592186412993 13194139534170 17592186045417 true] [17592186412997 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139534170 17592186045417 true] [17592186412997 1077 "selection" 13194139534170 17592186045417 true] [17592186412997 1078 #edb/ref 17592186412986 13194139534170 17592186045417 true] [17592186412997 1079 #uuid "f812c18b-3e31-5102-8677-d82c1a372eb4" 13194139534170 17592186045417 true] [17592186412998 1068 #edb/ref 17592186412995 13194139534170 17592186045417 true] [17592186412998 1069 "q-109277" 13194139534170 17592186045417 true] [17592186412998 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534170 17592186045417 true] [17592186412998 1071 "$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\overset{A+3I\\,}{\\to }v\\overset{A+3I\\,}{\\to }w\\overset{A+3I\\,}{\\to }0 \\\\ x\\overset{A-3I\\,}{\\to }y\\overset{A-3I\\,}{\\to }0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven the action of the matrices $A+3I$ and $A-3I$ on the nonzero vectors $u,v,w,x$ and $y$ as depicted above, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-3$ of $A$\n- $u$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-3$ of $A$\n- $y$ is an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =3$ of $A$" 13194139534170 17592186045417 true] [17592186412998 1072 #uuid "28941d92-3462-55d4-9376-780e240021c7" 13194139534170 17592186045417 true] [17592186412998 1074 "A *generalized eigenvector* ${v}_{n}$ of rank $n$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda $ of a matrix $A$ is a nonzero vector such that\n\n$$\n{v}_{n}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{n-1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }…\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\nwhere ${v}_{n-1},{v}_{n-2},…,{v}_{1}\\ne 0.$ An *eigenvector* is simply a generalized eigenvector of rank $1.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, since \n\n$$\nv\\overset{A+3I\\,}{\\to }w\\overset{A+3I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\ni.e. $A+3I$ maps $v$ to $0$ in $2$ steps, \nthe vector $v$ is a generalized  eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-3$ of $A.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since\n\n$$\nu\\overset{A+3I\\,}{\\to }v\\overset{A+3I\\,}{\\to }w\\overset{A+3I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\ni.e. $A+3I$ maps $u$ to $0$ in $3$ steps, \nthe vector $u$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $3,$ not $2.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, since\n\n$$\ny\\overset{A-3I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\ni.e. $A-3I$ maps $y$ to $0$ in $1$ step, \nthe vector $y$ is an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =3$ of $A.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139534170 17592186045417 true] [17592186412999 1068 #edb/ref 17592186412996 13194139534170 17592186045417 true] [17592186412999 1069 "q-109510" 13194139534170 17592186045417 true] [17592186412999 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534170 17592186045417 true] [17592186412999 1071 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}],\\,v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ and vector $v$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $v$ is an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2$ of $A$\n- $v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $1$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2$ of $A$\n- $v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2$ of $A$" 13194139534170 17592186045417 true] [17592186412999 1072 #uuid "5e5966d3-ce4d-5835-ab91-1f478e587602" 13194139534170 17592186045417 true] [17592186412999 1074 "A *generalized eigenvector* ${v}_{2}$ of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda $ of a matrix $A$ is a nonzero vector such that\n\n$$\n{v}_{2}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\nwhere ${v}_{1}\\ne 0.$ Recall that an *eigenvector* is simply a generalized eigenvector of rank $1.$\n\nWith this in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\nFirst, we compute $A-2I:$\n\n$$\nA-2I=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}]-2[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- Next, we find the image of $v$ under the action of $A-2I:$\n\n$$\n(A-2I)v=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]={{[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{w}\\ne 0\n$$\n\n- Since we got a nonzero vector in the last step, we now compute the image of $w$ under the action of $A-2I:$\n\n$$\n(A-2I)w=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=0\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain the following chain of maps:\n\n$$\n{{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{v}\\overset{\\,A-2I\\,\\,}{\\to }{{[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{w}\\overset{\\,A-2I\\,\\,}{\\to }{{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{0}\n$$\n\nHere, we see that $A-2I$ maps $v$ to $0$ in $2$ steps.\n\nTherefore:\n\n$\\,$ *$v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2$ of $A.$*\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139534170 17592186045417 true] [17592186413000 1068 #edb/ref 17592186412997 13194139534170 17592186045417 true] [17592186413000 1069 "q-109434" 13194139534170 17592186045417 true] [17592186413000 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534170 17592186045417 true] [17592186413000 1071 "$$\n\\begin{aligned}s\\overset{A-4I\\,}{\\to }u\\overset{A-4I\\,}{\\to }v\\overset{A-4I\\,}{\\to }w\\overset{A-4I\\,}{\\to }0 \\\\ x\\overset{\\,\\,\\,A\\,\\,\\,\\,}{\\to }y\\overset{\\,\\,\\,A\\,\\,\\,\\,}{\\to }0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven the action of the matrices $A-4I$ and $A$ on the nonzero vectors $s,u,v,w,x$ and $y$ as depicted above, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $s$ is a generalized  eigenvector of rank $4$  corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =4$ of $A$\n- $x$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $1$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =0$ of $A$\n- $v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $3$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =4$ of $A$" 13194139534170 17592186045417 true] [17592186413000 1072 #uuid "eddb46c6-735a-5bdf-b2a2-b57344c5441a" 13194139534170 17592186045417 true] [17592186413000 1074 "A *generalized eigenvector* ${v}_{n}$ of rank $n$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda $ of a matrix $A$ is a nonzero vector such that\n\n$$\n{v}_{n}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{n-1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }…\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\nwhere ${v}_{n-1},{v}_{n-2},…,{v}_{1}\\ne 0.$ An *eigenvector* is simply a generalized eigenvector of rank $1.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, since \n\n$$\ns\\overset{A-4I\\,}{\\to }u\\overset{A-4I\\,}{\\to }v\\overset{A-4I\\,}{\\to }w\\overset{A-4I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\ni.e. $A-4I$ maps $s$ to $0$ in $4$ steps, \nthe vector $s$ is an generalized eigenvector of rank $4$  corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =4$ of $A.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since\n\n$$\nx\\overset{\\,\\,\\,A\\,\\,\\,\\,}{\\to }y\\overset{\\,\\,\\,A\\,\\,\\,\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\ni.e. $A=A-0I$ maps $x$ to $0$ in $2$ steps, \nthe vector $x$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2,$ not $1.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since\n\n$$\nv\\overset{A-4I\\,}{\\to }w\\overset{A-4I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\ni.e. $A-4I$ maps $v$ to $0$ in $2$ steps, \nthe vector $v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2,$ not $3.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139534170 17592186045417 true] [17592186413001 1058 #edb/ref 17592186413003 13194139534170 17592186045417 true] [17592186413001 1062 #uuid "2bc8515b-cb7e-52c2-b2ca-3e1502bf57b3" 13194139534170 17592186045417 true] [17592186413001 1063 #edb/ref 17592186413000 13194139534170 17592186045417 true] [17592186413002 1058 #edb/ref 17592186413001 13194139534170 17592186045417 true] [17592186413002 1062 #uuid "ad6297d7-7ae6-5be0-a908-e7a0cc46d152" 13194139534170 17592186045417 true] [17592186413002 1063 #edb/ref 17592186412998 13194139534170 17592186045417 true] [17592186413003 1062 #uuid "ea9a4e19-af76-5650-86a8-7d1214ec77ba" 13194139534170 17592186045417 true] [17592186413003 1063 #edb/ref 17592186412999 13194139534170 17592186045417 true]] :edb/tempids {"activity-b5cf7ec7-b0e0-5d92-bb87-56ae58980cda" 17592186412979 "answer-3927cd36-e744-5c4b-b518-90f4314da95c" 17592186412980 "answer-3e0f7a4e-e2de-5221-a243-87d47db94856" 17592186412981 "answer-4520b82c-7699-585f-98d4-b020b73654d0" 17592186412982 "answer-551aa92b-df71-5900-93f2-5ef5c2f52582" 17592186412983 "answer-57f29804-0dce-5ac6-8836-fe816e8c4d5d" 17592186412984 "answer-5a0d3c84-3d53-59fb-b4db-6527f0bb894b" 17592186412985 "answer-7b712b8f-0b0e-5fc8-bf18-7ff7f27b35bd" 17592186412986 "answer-9865884d-6f2a-52d2-8326-716abac188bd" 17592186412987 "answer-bb216ff2-4b67-5cf4-8b40-eae9eda5614c" 17592186412988 "answer-be644adc-7532-5ed2-b8e4-b29d16f2b2a7" 17592186412989 "answer-c05a3db1-b2e4-555c-b799-5b9528462b9d" 17592186412990 "answer-d1e55130-8888-5317-8d53-8e631e8c4625" 1759218641299